+# Daily Grind rewards
+
+Requires Node.js 24+ and pnpm. Install with `pnpm install`; `pnpm build` performs syntax checks and `pnpm test` runs automated tests without opening a browser or starting a server. `pnpm start` serves the homepage and POST `/api/verify-email` together. A static file server cannot perform verification.
+
+Configure `APP_ORIGIN` to the public HTTPS origin, with no trailing slash, and `EMAIL_VERIFICATION_ORIGIN_TRIAL_TOKEN` to a valid token registered for that origin. The server inserts the trial token in the page; it does not manufacture a trial credential. Set these environment variables through your deployment platform (the example env file is documentation, not loaded automatically). Production requires an HTTPS `APP_ORIGIN`. Local HTTP defaults to `http://localhost:11222` but is not a substitute for an enrolled HTTPS origin when using EVP.
+
+The Email Verification API is currently experimental: consult [Chrome’s relying party documentation](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/identity/email-verification/verifier) for enrollment, supported versions, and trial expiration. Customers must use a participating browser/provider and be signed in to their email provider in that browser. No OTP or magic link is sent. Missing, expired, or invalid proofs leave the account uncreated and show retry instructions. This intentionally replaces the usual email fallback to satisfy the no-verification-email requirement.
+
+Each page render gets a random nonce stored server-side against an HttpOnly session cookie (Secure on HTTPS). Form challenges expire after 10 minutes and are consumed atomically on submission. Independent challenges support multiple tabs. The page is served with `Cache-Control: no-store`. Native form submission lets the browser fill the hidden token at submission time; no client-side token verification is used.
+
+Verification uses `@sd-jwt/core` and `jose`, with DNS issuer delegation, public HTTPS metadata/JWKS discovery, signature verification, verified-email matching, and required key binding (nonce, origin audience, issuance age, and SD hash). The verifier accepts both current `evt+jwt` and earlier trial `ev+sd-jwt` types, and asymmetric Ed25519/EdDSA/ES256 algorithms. Discovery blocks private network destinations and redirects. The SD-JWT options match the installed library API, including `keyBindingNonce`; using an unsupported options shape could silently skip key binding.
+
+Accounts and single-use challenges persist in SQLite at `DATABASE_PATH` (default `data/rewards.sqlite`). Names, verified emails, verification timestamps, and salted scrypt password hashes are stored; raw passwords and tokens are not. Email uniqueness is case insensitive. Deploy a single Node service with a persistent private data volume; replicas must share an atomic database rather than separate local SQLite files. Request limits use the direct connection IP; behind a reverse proxy they apply to the proxy address unless adapted to trusted infrastructure. Existing account/menu links are outside this registration feature; no login or rewards redemption system is added here.
